# Choice specifications: prompt, uniquely correct option, misconception-based wrong options with explanations.
Q={}
def q(key,prompt,right,*wrong): Q[key]=(prompt,right,wrong)
q('026_026.s03','Choose the collection types, in order: (1) ordered quantities that must be updated; (2) a fixed immutable ordered pair; (3) distinct labels for membership tests.','list; tuple; set',('tuple; list; set','A tuple is immutable, whereas the first requirement needs updates; the second calls for an immutable tuple.'),('list; tuple; list','A list is a positional sequence, not the distinct-value collection specified for membership.'))
q('026_026.s04','Quantities must be updated and retrieved by item label, not by position. Which collection meets that requirement?','A dictionary mapping labels to quantities.',('A tuple of quantities.','A tuple is immutable and provides sequence positions, not label-based lookup.'),('A set of quantities.','A set stores distinct values, not a mapping from labels to quantities.'),('A list of quantities accessed by position.','The requirement is lookup by label, not by sequence position.'))
q('027_028.s02','Which print call separates two values with a colon and space and ends with an exclamation mark followed by a newline?','print("Selected", "pen", sep=": ", end="!\\n")',('print("Selected", "pen")','The defaults are a space between values and a newline at the end, not the requested delimiters.'),('print("Selected", "pen", sep="!\\n", end=": ")','sep separates the values; end terminates the output. These arguments are reversed.'))
q('027_028.s03','Ada is entered at the prompt. Which two lines store the entered text in label and then display Hello Ada with one space and a final newline?','label = input("Name: ")\nprint("Hello", label)',('label = input("Name: ")\nprint("Hello", label, sep="")','An empty separator removes the required space.'),('label = input("Name: ")\nprint("Hello", label, end="")','An empty end removes the required final newline.'))
q('027_028.s06','Which loop header numbers labels from one while obtaining both the counter and the corresponding element?','for position, label in enumerate(labels, start=1):',('for position, label in enumerate(labels):','The default counter begins at zero, not one.'),('for position, label in enumerate(labels, start=2):','This starts at two, not the specified one.'))
q('029_029.s02','A computed decimal sum is to be compared within a tolerance, rather than for exact equality. Which comparison is appropriate?','math.isclose(a, b)',('a == b','Exact equality does not apply a tolerance; binary floating-point representations can be approximate.'),('Always declare the values unequal without comparing them.','Approximate representation does not mean every pair is unequal; isclose compares using tolerances.'))
q('030_030.s03','A loop is at the outer level. An if condition must belong to the loop, its addition must belong to the condition, and the final report must run after the loop. Using four spaces per level, choose their leading-space counts in that order.','4, 8, 0',('0, 4, 0','The condition at zero spaces would be outside the loop rather than in its suite.'),('4, 4, 4','The addition must be deeper than the condition, and the final report must be dedented out of the loop.'))
q('030_030.s04','Read the block structure:\n```python\nfor quantity in [3, 2]:\n    if quantity > 0:\n        total = total + quantity\n    print(total)\n```\nWhere does print(total) belong?','Inside the loop, but outside the if suite.',('Inside both the loop and the if suite.','The print is dedented from the if body, so it is not part of that deeper suite.'),('Outside the loop.','It still has one indentation level beneath the loop header.'))
q('031_031.s03','After import math, what happens when math.sqrt(-1.0) is evaluated?','It raises ValueError because this math operation is invalid for that real input.',('It returns a complex square root.','The math functions here operate on real rather than complex values.'),('It returns the square root of 1.0 instead.','sqrt does not discard the negative sign; the negative real argument is invalid.'))
q('032_034.s03','For order = [{"quantities": [3, 2]}], which assignment changes only the second quantity of the first order to 5?','order[0]["quantities"][1] = 5',('order[0]["quantities"][0] = 5','Index zero selects the first quantity, not the second.'),('order[0]["quantities"] = 5','This replaces the entire quantities list, rather than its second entry.'))
q('032_034.s06','For rows = [[3, 2], [5, 4]], an outer loop uses for row in rows: and its inner loop uses for cell in row:. What is the cell traversal order?','3, 2, 5, 4',('3, 5, 2, 4','The inner loop finishes the current row before proceeding to the next row; this interleaves rows.'),('3, 2, 3, 2','The outer loop advances to the second row; it does not always traverse the first row.'))
q('032_034.s09','Which description of range(6) is correct?','It starts at zero and uses step one.',('It starts at one and uses step one.','With one stop argument, the default start is zero.'),('It starts at zero and uses step zero.','The default step is one; range requires a nonzero step.'))
q('032_034.s10','Which range produces decreasing integers from 5 through 1, excluding zero?','range(5, 0, -1)',('range(5, 1, -1)','The stop is excluded, so stopping at 1 leaves out 1.'),('range(5, 0, 1)','A positive step does not move down from 5 toward the smaller stop.'))
q('035_037.s03','A script prints Ready and requests exit status 2. Which statement correctly distinguishes these observations?','Ready is standard output; 2 is a separate nonzero process status indicating abnormal termination.',('Ready is the exit status and 2 is printed text.','print sends text to standard output; the process status is a separate result.'),('Printing Ready guarantees exit status zero.','Printed output does not determine the requested process status.'))
q('035_037.s06','Read quantity = 3; cost = quantity * 4 + 2; quantity = 5 (as three statements in that order). Does the last assignment recalculate cost?','No. cost keeps the previously evaluated result; quantity is rebound to 5.',('Yes. cost is a live formula that updates whenever quantity changes.','Assignment evaluates its right-hand expression before binding a value; it does not store a live formula.'),('No. The last assignment cannot change quantity once it has a value.','Assignment can rebind quantity; it is cost that retains its earlier result.'))
q('035_037.s09','The fixed fee applies only when at least one item is present. Which comment accurately explains the reason for if quantity >= 1: before cost = quantity * 4 + 2?','# Exclude zero so no fixed fee is charged when no items are present.',('# Compare quantity with one.','This merely restates the comparison instead of explaining its non-obvious purpose.'),('# Charge the fixed fee even when there are no items.','That contradicts the quantity check and the stated fee rule.'))
q('038_040.s02','In an interactive tutorial, >>> quantity = 3 is followed by >>> quantity * 4 and then 12 without a prompt. Which text is a command to type?','quantity * 4, without >>>',('>>> quantity * 4, including >>>','Prompt characters mark interpreter input and must not be copied into the command.'),('12, because the line has no prompt.','The unprompted line is displayed output in this tutorial, not a command.'))
q('038_040.s06','Which statement accurately describes compilation of valid source in CPython?','CPython compiles source to bytecode used by its interpreter; bytecode is an implementation detail.',('Every Python implementation must use CPython bytecode.','The bytecode description is an implementation detail, not a cross-implementation guarantee.'),('Invalid syntax compiles successfully and is ignored at runtime.','Invalid source syntax raises SyntaxError rather than producing an executable code object.'))
q('038_040.s07','A script executes quantity = 3, then cost = quantity * 4. Which trace correctly explains the second binding?','quantity is bound first; cost then uses its current value to compute and bind the result.',('cost is bound before the right-hand expression is evaluated.','Assignment evaluates its expression before binding the result.'),('The compiler binds cost to a value without executing the assignments.','Evaluation and binding of these assignments occur at runtime.'))
q('038_040.s10','Which formatting preserves both print arguments and uses implicit continuation with a hanging indent?','print(\n    "Cost",\n    cost\n)',('print\n    ("Cost", cost)','The open parenthesis must enclose the continued call; this splits before the delimiter opens.'),('print(\n    cost,\n    "Cost"\n)','Although enclosed, this reverses the arguments rather than preserving the call.'))
q('041_041.s03','Which expression removes surrounding whitespace and converts text to lowercase, retaining the returned normalized string?','normalized = text.strip().lower()',('text.strip()\ntext.lower()','The returned strings are discarded; these calls do not modify the original string.'),('normalized = text.lower()','Lowercasing alone leaves the surrounding whitespace in place.'))
q('042_042.s02','An if header is missing its colon and its body contains int("three"). What must happen before tracing execution of that source?','Repair the missing colon so the source can compile.',('Trace the conversion first; invalid syntax does not prevent execution.','Invalid syntax prevents compilation to an executable code object.'),('Treat the missing colon as a runtime ValueError.','The missing colon is a parsing failure; ValueError from conversion belongs to execution.'))
q('043_044.s02','Which pair has false truth value followed by true truth value?','An empty list, then a list containing 3.',('A list containing 0, then an empty list.','Nonempty built-in lists are true even when the element is zero; an empty list is false.'),('An empty dictionary, then an empty tuple.','Both are empty collections and therefore false.'))
q('043_044.s03','Which scalar pair has false truth value followed by true truth value?','0, then 3.',('3, then 0.','Nonzero numbers are true; zero is false, so this reverses the required order.'),('None, then False.','Both None and False have false truth values.'))
q('043_044.s06','quantity may be None (absent) or a legitimate zero. Which condition tests only absence?','quantity is None',('not quantity','A truth test also treats zero as false, so it does not isolate absence.'),('quantity is not None','This tests that quantity is supplied, the opposite of the requested absence test.'))
q('043_044.s07','Why use quantity is not None rather than if quantity: to recognize a supplied quantity that may be zero?','The identity test accepts zero as supplied and rejects only None.',('Because zero and None are the same object.','None represents absence; a legitimate numeric zero is not None.'),('Because truth testing considers zero true.','Zero has false truth value, which is why the truth test would reject it.'))
q('045_045.s03','Which pair of operations recovers Unicode text after converting it to UTF-8 bytes?','payload = text.encode("utf-8")\nrestored = payload.decode("utf-8")',('payload = text.decode("utf-8")\nrestored = payload.encode("utf-8")','Encoding converts text to bytes; decoding converts bytes back to text. These directions are reversed.'),('payload = text.encode("utf-8")\nrestored = payload','payload is bytes, not the decoded Unicode text required for restored.'))
q('045_045.s04','A bytes payload is not valid for the chosen decoding encoding. What failure does decoding report?','UnicodeDecodeError',('It always recovers the original text regardless of encoding.','The decoding encoding must match the data, and invalid bytes can cause a decoding failure.'),('It silently treats bytes as already-decoded Unicode text.','bytes and str are distinct; decoding can fail instead of returning text.'))

